body: |-
  ## Where things stand

  The denominator is still ZERO. No F4 run has been scored. What changed is that the instrument is now honest enough to know why.

  ### Shipped this session (3 commits, all local on warpline-surfaces)

  **97fb108b — #f4-cli-trace, the CLI arm becomes measurable.** cli.ts emitted no f4Trace rows at all, so the "both skins independently" clause of the pass bar could not be evaluated. Beyond wiring traceCli over fork/propose/admit(all 3 engine paths)/knot show/grade/refs list/status/`daemon call`, the load-bearing fix was seq: the classifier ORDERS BY SEQ, F4Tracer counted seq per PROCESS, and the CLI is one process per command — so every CLI row would have carried seq:0 and the transcript would have been unorderable. seq is now seeded per RUN from the trace file. Rows name the DAEMON VERB so the classifier stays arm-agnostic; CLI-only commands take a `cli:` prefix (warpline status = 'cli:status', the meaning diff, NOT the daemon's cycle-position status). test/f4-cli-arm.test.ts drives the real binary over six processes and returns the same episode shape as the MCP arm.

  **64574657 — FG-1 RATIFIED (TD-2026-07-28-168).** The falsifier as written demanded the cold agent complete `resolve`, which protocol.ts:112 forbids to agent-class principals, which admit.ts:336-353 orders ESCALATION for instead, and which classifier.ts W3 scores as WASTED. Unsatisfiable for reasons unrelated to legibility. Ratified in amended form because the originally-pinned wording ("emits a structured escalation") is not observable in f4Trace — there is no escalate verb, so scoring it would need a human read per transcript and would break the pure-code classifier discipline. Recorded consequence: git resolution parity is deliberately out of scope (contested meaning is decided by a human — the accountability moat), not conceded.

  **04fb8ec8 — FG-3 pre-freeze review, two defects fixed.** (1) descriptorsId hashed VERB_DESCRIPTORS alone while PW-6 had deliberately relocated the F4 teaching INTO the status RESULT (hosts truncate descriptions; nothing truncates a result) — so the freeze would have pinned the wrong artifact and a next-verb-rule edit would have changed what every cold agent is taught while the id, and the >=10-run denominator, stood still. The rule is now declarative data in descriptors.ts and the id hashes verb table + rule + derived tool-name map. (2) Probed LIVE: after a KNOT, status answered nextLegalVerbs ['admit'] while the refusal's own ladder said [knot.show, resolve(human)] — steering a re-orienting agent into the identical re-admit the classifier scores W1, expensive because the classifier grants exactly ONE orientation call per episode. Fixed via position.knotOpen. New id descriptors:v1:445e4eb7…

  ### Then the panel (e21054d2)

  Six specialists — Jinx, Loid, Aegis, Arky, Shield, North. Every finding independently re-verified against the code or the live fabric before recording; unverified claims dropped. Nine defects. The three that block the remaining gates:

  - **D-1** FG-1 predicate (2) is UNSATISFIABLE on the byte-downgrade stratum. Three of four KNOT sites never emit a payloadId (admit.ts:830/867 byte-overlap, 779 shadow); only :931 does. Without a pointer the ladder degrades to the human door alone, so the agent cannot hydrate however well it behaves — and FG-4 names that stratum as REQUIRED. As ratified it would fail the bar on wording, not on agents. The exact failure FG-1 existed to remove, reintroduced one layer down.
  - **D-2** "zero W3" cannot fire on the MCP arm at all: `resolve` is unregistered and throws BEFORE tracer.emit (no row exists), and acceptBreach/acceptRisk are absent from admit's paramsSchema so filterToSchema drops them and the classifier's /accept(Breach|Risk)/ target test can never match. A criterion that cannot fail is not a gate.
  - **D-3** the refusal LADDERS are still outside descriptorsId — the same defect the FG-3 review just fixed, one level up. Signing today pre-registers ~2/3 of the teaching surface while attesting to all of it.

  Plus: D-4 CLI `resolve` untraced AND ungated (a CLI subject can self-resolve its own KNOT and satisfy all three FG-1 predicates); D-5 the >=10/>=80% bar is statistically hollow (8/10 Wilson CI [0.49, 0.94] — cannot separate 0.65 from 0.80); D-6 the ladder defect class is dense (CLAIM_BREACH ladder is an infinite loop; fork-clobber steers a KNOTted agent back to admit, contradicting the fix just landed); D-7 no --root anywhere, so an in-repo scratch fabric resolves via repoRoot() to the LIVE fabric and T-005 specifies fixtures inside the repo; D-8 the trace is blind to the mistakes it counts, biasing every number TOWARD passing monotonically with how bad the surface is; D-9 verified live — 33 shadow rows (19 FAST_ADMIT, 14 NOOP, 0 CLEAN, 0 KNOT), all 60 strands authoredBy.agentId null, gate.agentWrites:'real' ON and never fired.

  ### How the panel's split resolved itself

  North ranked "sign FG-3 after a bounded probe" #1 with the falsifier "unless the probe finds a third teaching carrier outside the hash" — the probe found one (D-3). North also flagged a full audit as theater "unless the probe turns up one further carrier/ladder contradiction" — Jinx found two (D-6). Both falsifiers tripped, converging on Arky's BOUNDED audit form. The pre-registered-falsifier discipline did the arbitration, not seniority and not consensus.

  ### The strategic reframe (North)

  F4 and the empty denominator are the same problem wearing two hats: both need a concurrent second principal, and seed.ts (seedWorld + rivalAdvance) is already a fleet in miniature driving the real engine functions. Build the runner as the CONCURRENCY DRIVER whose first consumer is F4, then re-point it at the live fabric with per-agent worktrees to start the organic K3 clock. Scoped narrower, North's own case against F4-first becomes correct.

  Second, not previously in the record: F4 is not a UX gate, it is the test of the moat-silence STRATEGY. If cold agents of other providers cannot work the surface from runtime self-description alone, then "never published, never in weights" is not a defensible moat but a fatal distribution decision, and the founder must choose between publishing and a much more human-in-the-loop product. That fork is unavailable at n=0.

  ### Recommended sequence (pre-freeze; each invalidates the denominator if it lands later)

  1. Amend FG-1 for D-1/D-2 (correct-door restatement; make W3 measurable).
  2. Widen the freeze to an instrumentId over descriptors ‖ ladder text ‖ classifier rule-set ‖ CLI_VERB_MAP ‖ corpus hash — freeze the carrier, not a proxy for it.
  3. Bounded ladder audit: over corpus-reachable refusal classes × reachable CyclePosition states, assert P1 non-contradiction and P2 human-door safety, as a green CI extension of PW-4.
  4. Implement the FG-1 completion predicate in code — it does not exist in src/f4/ today; the >=80% PRIMARY metric has no implementation, only the secondary wasted-turn metric does.
  5. --root + the runner's scratch assertion.
  6. Then set FG-4 (corrected strata + counts), then sign the widened freeze.

  ### Correction carried forward

  cli-trace.ts:26-28 asserts that closing the three CLI asymmetries moves descriptorsId and costs the denominator. It does not — projecting the existing daemon status result through a CLI command, adding shadow tail, and making CLI --claim optional touch none of the three hashed inputs. They were escalated to the founder as expensive decisions on a false premise. Comment needs fixing.
